{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '1345,1361p;2078,2110p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1,31p;279,305p;820,855p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md | sed -n '40,85p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1345\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1346\t| `:vm/park`    | `:park`                | `#{}`:             | —                                                                                                        |\n  1347\t|               |                        | scheduler-internal |                                                                                                          |\n  1348\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1349\t| `:vm/resume   | `:resume pid`          | `#{}`              | `pid` into the parked-record obligation                                                                  |\n  1350\t| pid`          |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1351\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1352\t| `:vm/gensym`  | `:gensym`              | `#{}`: the id      | —                                                                                                        |\n  1353\t|               |                        | counter is machine |                                                                                                          |\n  1354\t|               |                        | state              |                                                                                                          |\n  1355\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1356\t| `:vm/store-ge | `:store-get k`,        | `#{}`              | `k` into the store-slice requirement                                                                     |\n  1357\t| t k`,         | `:store-put k`         |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1358\t| `:vm/store-pu |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1359\t| t k`          |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1360\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1361\t| a definition  | `:define k`            | `#{}`: the write   | `k` into the store-slice requirement, as a `:store-put` key: every definition key is literal             |\n  2078\t   random token, never a log-allocated id or a stream position; its\n  2079\t   uniqueness is the composition's warranty, discharged by the token's\n  2080\t   randomness, and no transactor is claimed to provide it. The\n  2081\t   `:incarnation` field of `ctx` and the token-minting step at\n  2082\t   construction are additions to `yin.vm.macro.md`'s `ctx` and expander\n  2083\t   construction that this document cannot make there.\n  2084\t4. **Macro batch and admission preservation** (§8.5) — design work with\n  2085\t   stated mechanisms. The ordered batch, the admission-order harvest\n  2086\t   catalogue of occurrence groups, the derived macro-only declaration\n  2087\t   catalogue, its admission validation, the validate-before-strip rule,\n  2088\t   and the adapter's path-and-entity tracking during inlining must exist\n  2089\t   before any tuple program containing a macro definition can be\n  2090\t   admitted. The legacy datom-batch contract is\n  2091\t   preserved only through the adapter-derived catalogue; tuple-native\n  2092\t   frontends operate\n  2093\t   under the separate declaration-order contract stated there. The v2\n  2094\t   expander itself does not exist yet (`src/cljc/yin/vm/` has no\n  2095\t   `macro.cljc`) and is not a blocker for tuple evaluation without\n  2096\t   macros; §8.4's event shape is specified against `yin.vm.macro.md`, not\n  2097\t   against code.\n  2098\t5. **Conservative dependency completion** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3) — design\n  2099\t   work with stated mechanisms. Free-name discharge requires searching the\n  2100\t   store slice by key or resolving via primitive/module profiles (§7.7.2);\n  2101\t   convergence is over work items and all dependency facts, with the\n  2102\t   captured-environment context abstraction for values. Neither analysis exists in code;\n  2103\t   until they do, an emitter may only report `:incomplete`. Extracting the\n  2104\t   free-name set itself must use an occurrence-joined query, not a\n  2105\t   row-only one, to stay conservative when a name is free at one\n  2106\t   occurrence and bound at another (§4.5).\n  2107\t6. **Effect normalization** (§7.7.1): the footprint table must be\n  2108\t   published with the execution-contract stamp, and the tree/segment\n  2109\t   requirement-set equality must be tested over the corpus. FFI ops are a\n  2110\t   receiver capability requirement, never a store-slice requirement.\n     1\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\n     2\t\n     3\t> [!WARNING]  \n     4\t> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is\n     5\t> theoretically sound as an architecture-agnostic continuation format, but\n     6\t> true heterogeneous network migration requires addressing several critical\n     7\t> defects identified in the 2026-09-14 architectural review. This revision\n     8\t> (r4) incorporates r2's answers to that review's findings 1-19, the owner\n     9\t> ruling on S7.3's canonical form (r3), and Rule R's \"v3\" contract (r4);\n    10\t> S7.11 lists the acceptance blockers that keep this warning standing until\n    11\t> an implementation phase closes each of them with tests.\n    12\t\n    13\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) is proposed as the canonical exchange format for network-transparent continuations. Because this lowered representation resolves execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST, it provides a simpler target for specialized execution engines (e.g., WebAssembly, LLVM, hardware FPGA) to participate in the `datom.world` ecosystem.\n    14\t\n    15\tTo safely \"lift on park\" and \"lower on resume\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\n    16\t\n    17\t1. **Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:** `:yin.code/source` mappings are insufficient for state reconstruction. The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions — including effect-producing `:call`s — and reconstruction metadata must separate what is derivable from the canonical code alone (stack *effects*, lexical *requirements*) from what is per-activation state only the frame can carry (absolute depths, captured environments). Foreign engines publish per-segment safepoint maps derived from the canonical stream so arbitrary hardware states can be predictably lifted into the canonical CESK format.\n    18\t2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\n    19\t3. **Dependency Closure & Context:** A parked fragment is not a complete configuration. The format must carry its complete scheduler context — required primitives *with profiles*, loaded modules, referenced parked records, the fresh-name counter, and the reachable store slice — discovered by a conservative fixed point that includes explicit store operands, and must restore into an **isolated execution store** so the receiver's own state cannot change resolution.\n    20\t4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. Exclusion requires a **grounded authority** — a resource the grantor's boundary actually possesses, named in the value, independent of carriers — plus an **exporting state** that fences the source before publication, and **effect fencing** with authority epochs and stable operation ids, because absence of a lapse record is never evidence of tenure.\n    21\t5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles — a **canonical instruction vector** (positional tuples, not EAV) whose address is computed over the loader's resolved interpretation, whose addressed payload is the vector itself, and whose interpretation is pinned by a contract stamp versioning the *complete* execution contract.\n    22\t\n    23\t---\n    24\t\n    25\t# Universal Continuation Format — protocol draft\n    26\t\n    27\tStatus: Phase 5 design draft, 2026-09-14; revised 2026-09-14 (r2) per the\n    28\tarchitectural review `collab/1789387292000-architect-ucf-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`;\n    29\tamended 2026-09-14 (r3) by owner ruling — §7.3's canonical form is the\n    30\tpositional instruction tuple vector, not EAV (§7.3.2).\n    31\tAmended 2026-09-25 (r4) for Rule R, `yin/def` is syntax, never a name\n   279\t```\n   280\t\n   281\tA resumer whose loader implements a different revision does not guess: it\n   282\treturns `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (§7.9) *before* lowering. Adding an opcode,\n   283\tchanging a transition, or changing the resolution order is a new revision; a\n   284\tresumer on the old one cannot run code that observes the difference, and the\n   285\tstamp is how it finds out before lowering rather than at an unknown integer\n   286\tin `case`. Two revisions may be declared compatible only by a published\n   287\tstatement in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4, never by an engine's own judgment.\n   288\tBecause interpretation is part of identity, an engine's code index is keyed\n   289\t**per stamp revision**: lookup is `(get-in index [stamp address])`, and the\n   290\tsame address under different stamps is a different image the same way the\n   291\tsame datoms under different loaders are a different program.\n   292\t\n   293\tThe current revision is \"v3\" (`yin.vm/semantic-contract`, and\n   294\t`yin.vm/ast-contract` for the Universal AST). It superseded \"v2\" because\n   295\tRule R changed the resolution order and added the `:define` opcode and\n   296\ttransition; see the revision log in S7.11. The de Bruijn images carry\n   297\ttheir own names, \"b2\" (stack) and \"r2\" (register). Every persistent-code\n   298\tloader requires a stamp and refuses `:contract-missing` or\n   299\t`:contract-mismatch` before validation, so an old-stamped image fails by\n   300\tstamp, not by grammar. The lowering adapters (`linearize/ast-loader`,\n   301\t`linearize/rows-loader`) and `ucf/canonicalize` are not exceptions:\n   302\teach requires its input's own stamp and verifies it before lowering or\n   303\tcanonicalizing, and stamps only output it produced from verified input.\n   304\tFresh-code producers (yang, the expander, `vm/eval`, a linearizer over\n   305\tcode it just lowered) supply the current constant themselves, and an\n   820\t  where the emitter holds the batch, as canonical tuples where only a vector\n   821\t  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`\n   822\t  operands**, which name store\n   823\t  keys directly and which the first draft missed entirely; every `:var`\n   824\t  name; every effect op (`:stream-*`, `:ffi-call`, and the effects of\n   825\t  `:effectful`-class primitives the walk finds); every `:resume` operand\n   826\t  (a parked id, §7.6.3). Discovering a new work item continues the fixed point\n   827\t  with that address and context even when the address was already walked for\n   828\t  another context.\n   829\t- **Names:** only a free `:var` name is an obligation. An obligation is\n   830\t  discharged by a matching key in the reachable store slice, or retained as\n   831\t  a required primitive or module export and checked by profile (§7.5.2). An\n   832\t  environment never discharges a name: captured environments contribute\n   833\t  values to their work item's reachability analysis, while lexical binding\n   834\t  determines whether a `:var` is free before it becomes an obligation. If no\n   835\t  store key or profiled primitive/module requirement can discharge an\n   836\t  obligation, discovery is `:incomplete`; after lowering, an unavailable\n   837\t  retained requirement is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` (§7.6.5).\n   838\t  Definitions are syntax (Rule R, S7.5.2): the definition operator\n   839\t  `yin/def` is never a free name and never an obligation, because a\n   840\t  well-formed segment has no `:var` naming it (`free-names` never returns\n   841\t  it); a `[:define name]` operand is a store key the segment writes, like\n   842\t  a `:store-put` operand, not a name it resolves.\n   843\t- **Modules:** a module's manifest declares its exported primitive profiles\n   844\t  and its **store footprint** (store keys and effect kinds its handlers may\n   845\t  touch). Until manifests carry footprints, any required module whose\n   846\t  footprint is undeclared sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` — the honest\n   847\t  statement that closure could not be computed, never a silent\n   848\t  under-approximation. A module effect handler may read store keys the\n   849\t  static walk cannot see; a composition may supply extra slice keys\n   850\t  explicitly, and the value records that it did.\n   851\t\n   852\tBecause the computation is over data, the same query answers \"what does this\n   853\tprogram need?\" for a segment that has never run. And because discovery can\n   854\t*fail to terminate successfully* — a missing segment cannot be walked, an\n   855\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\n    40\t`:parked`, `:ready-queue`, and `:wait-set` looking for `:compiled-version`\n    41\tinside `:next`-chained frames. On a payload without those keys they find\n    42\tnothing, which is correct; no VM in this repository uses the compiled cache.\n    43\t\n    44\t### 1.1 Names and the program store (Rule R)\n    45\t\n    46\t`yin/def` is syntax, never a name. Two engine functions carry that rule\n    47\tfor every VM:\n    48\t\n    49\t    resolve-var      env store primitives registry name -> value\n    50\t    store-put        store key val -> store       (refuses a reserved key)\n    51\t\n    52\t`resolve-var` refuses a reserved name (`:reserved-name`, role\n    53\t`:variable`) before it consults env or store; every executing variable\n    54\tlookup of all four VMs goes through it. `store-put`, with its guard\n    55\t`check-store-key!`, is the one program store write: the `:vm/store-put`\n    56\teffect dispatcher, the direct store instructions of the walker, semantic,\n    57\tstack, and register VMs, and all four definition transitions write\n    58\tthrough it, and none of those transitions resolves its operator. A\n    59\tprogram therefore cannot redirect a definition through any binding.\n    60\t\n    61\t`resume-from-run-queue` asserts that a ready entry's `:store-updates`\n    62\tcarries only keyword keys, the ones the engine mints, and refuses any\n    63\tother key with `{:rule :store-update-key}`.\n    64\t\n    65\tEvery other store write in the tree is state construction and sits on an\n    66\texact allowlist, enforced by the store-write audit\n    67\t(`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`). The audit reads every file\n    68\tunder `src` as forms, every host's reader-conditional branch included,\n    69\tand finds each store mutation: an `assoc`, `assoc-in`, `update`,\n    70\t`update-in`, `merge`, `merge-with`, `into`, `dissoc`, `select-keys`,\n    71\t`conj`, `swap!`, `reset!`, `vswap!`, or `vreset!` whose argument is\n    72\t`:store`, whose key path contains `:store`, or whose target is a store;\n    73\tevery `store-put` call; and every map literal with a `:store` key. It\n    74\tfails on any site, keyed by file, top-level form, and head, outside the\n    75\tlist, on any listed site that is gone, and on an unreadable file;\n    76\tnegative fixtures pin the common write forms it must catch. The list:\n    77\tprogram writes through `engine/store-put`; engine-minted keyword keys\n    78\t(stream ids, cursor ids, and cursor advance in `handle-next` and the\n    79\twait-set resolver); the ready-queue merge (asserted keyword keys); VM\n    80\tconstruction (the FFI pair, and the two de Bruijn constructor merges,\n    81\tchecked); `dao.await` (checked keyword keys); the REPL history keys\n    82\t`*1`, `*2`, `*3`; the continuation handoff demo (checked); a JVM demo\n    83\tstore transfer (`src/clj/yin/demo.clj`); display projections in two\n    84\tbrowser demos; the waitset result and a dao.space index handle, which\n    85\tare not VM program writes; and the expander's macro store. A new store\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
